A||||20||Hippocrat||cutting tool of choice?||||5||
Z||000000||Hippocrat||10-08-2001||04:05 AM||||What is the best tool to use when cutting Fiero panels? <P>What gives the best cut on <BR>The hood?<BR>The fenders?  <BR>The decklid ?  <BR>The Fascia?<BR>as I understand certain parts a made from different materials?<P>Looking to do some custom mods and need the right tool for some precision cutting (very small and tight in spots) and it has to be a clean cut. <P>Im thinking what .. a dremmel(sp?) but what kind of bit..<P>Any Ideas out there?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||10-08-2001||07:16 AM||||I have been considering the same thing.  I know they make a grout remover tool for the Dremel (kind of a small router). Looks like it may be good for the softer panels.<BR>Gary||||reg||3
Z||000002||Cooter||10-08-2001||09:09 AM||||I usually use a "cut off tool"- an air powered grinder like tool that uses very thin oxide wheels. It slices through most everything. In other cases, a Makita Sawzall works nice too. Get an assortment of blades and lengths and start kicking up some dust! A die grinder- like a bigger Dremel- with some flapper wheels and de-burring bits helps with making sharp corners. Be sure to wear a mask and glasses because the SMC dust is a nasty irritant to nose/mouth/lungs and eyes.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||10-08-2001||10:10 AM||||Cooter's got the right tools!!!! Also the RotoZip has tons of accessories now! good for corners and such. DeWalt has a "Cut Out" tool and a laminate trimmer. The RotoZip and Cut out tool use the same bit= "Sabrecut" for fiberglass, plastic, vinyl siding & laminates about $60.00 to $100.00<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||3
Z||000004||Joe Torma||10-08-2001||10:15 AM||||I just used a jig saw to cut a hole in my hood, worked fine.  Cutoff tools work nice, but make lots of dust.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||10-08-2001||10:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DXR_DAD:<BR><B>Cooter's got the right tools!!!! Also the RotoZip has tons of accessories now! good for corners and such. DeWalt has a "Cut Out" tool and a laminate trimmer. The RotoZip and Cut out tool use the same bit= "Sabrecut" for fiberglass, plastic, vinyl siding & laminates about $60.00 to $100.00<P>DXR_DAD</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was just about to mention the Rotozip. My dad just picked one up a couple weeks ago. It's awesome! You can do so much with it. I forgot what he paid, but if my dad bought it, you can be sure it's worth the money  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">            <BR>^^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^^<BR><LI><FONT SIZE="1">84 SE (FOR SALE)*SOLD*<BR><LI>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR><LI>86 SC</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||10-08-2001||11:34 AM||||I have a RotoZip and it is a great tool.  It does grinding cutting and with the right bit should cut the SMC stuff without a problem.  I am doing a rebody and it has to be the tool I use the most.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DXR_DAD||10-08-2001||11:38 AM||||My hood vents were done with RotoZip and Air powered cut off tool:<P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=11236669&p=39805563&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||3
Z||000008||skitime||10-08-2001||11:50 AM||||I have cut quite a few holes my Fiero doing my custom work and have several more to make. The tools mentioned above all work great. The one tool I haven't seen mentioned is the wood router.  I have used the router on several projects.  It is great when you want a hole with a curved edge. It was used for the opening around the extra grill in the trunk and also for making the opening for the driving lights in the chin spoiler.  It does make a mess and wear eye and breathing protection at all times especially with fiberglass. It really works great on the plastic panels.  <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jefrysuko||10-08-2001||03:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by skitime:<BR><B>I have used the router on several projects.  It is great when you want a hole with a curved edge. It was used for the opening around the extra grill in the trunk and also for making the opening for the driving lights in the chin spoiler.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Would you happen to have some good close up pictures so we can see the results?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wolfhound||10-08-2001||04:18 PM||||You can also purchase an accessory router base for a Dremel tool for tight spots.<BR>here is a link: <A HREF="http://www.mytoolstore.com/dremel/router.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mytoolstore.com/dremel/router.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 10-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||skitime||10-08-2001||04:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B> Would you happen to have some good close up pictures so we can see the results?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Here you go.  Edges done with a router.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P000817-1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P000821-1.jpg"> <P><BR>||||reg||1
